Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function method_type_to_string ( method_type ) result ( method_str ) !! Convert method type integer constant to string !! !! Provides human-readable string representation of method type. integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: method_type !! Input integer constant character ( len = :), allocatable :: method_str !! Output string representation select case ( method_type ) ! Semi-empirical case ( METHOD_TYPE_GFN1 ) method_str = \"gfn1\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_GFN2 ) method_str = \"gfn2\" ! SCF methods case ( METHOD_TYPE_HF ) method_str = \"hf\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_DFT ) method_str = \"dft\" ! Multi-reference case ( METHOD_TYPE_MCSCF ) method_str = \"mcscf\" ! Effective fragment potentials case ( METHOD_TYPE_EFP2 ) method_str = \"efp2\" ! Perturbation theory case ( METHOD_TYPE_MP2 ) method_str = \"mp2\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_MP2_F12 ) method_str = \"mp2-f12\" ! Coupled cluster case ( METHOD_TYPE_CCSD ) method_str = \"ccsd\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_CCSD_T ) method_str = \"ccsd(t)\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_CCSD_F12 ) method_str = \"ccsd-f12\" case ( METHOD_TYPE_CCSD_T_F12 ) method_str = \"ccsd(t)-f12\" case default method_str = \"unknown\" end select end function method_type_to_string","tags":"","url":"proc/method_type_to_string.html"},{"title":"method_wants_density_fitting – metalquicha","text":"public  function method_wants_density_fitting(method_str) result(wants_df) Whether a method name asks for the fitted route “ri-mp2” and “df-mp2” parse to METHOD_TYPE_MP2 exactly as “mp2” does,\nso without this a deck asking for either would run the conventional\nfour-index transform and report it as RI – right answer, wrong method,\nand no way to tell from the output. Same reason method_spin_scaling exists: the spelling carries intent the type discards. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: method_str Return Value logical Called by proc~~method_wants_density_fitting~~CalledByGraph proc~method_wants_density_fitting method_wants_density_fitting proc~populate_config populate_config proc~populate_config->proc~method_wants_density_fitting proc~read_json_config_file read_json_config_file proc~read_json_config_file->proc~populate_config proc~read_json_config_text read_json_config_text proc~read_json_config_text->proc~populate_config proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~read_json_config_text proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~read_json_config_text program~main main program~main->proc~read_json_config_file proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~worker_loop~2->proc~worker_run proc~session_begin mqc_session_t%session_begin proc~session_begin->proc~worker_loop~2 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: i character(len=:), private, allocatable :: lower_str Source Code function method_wants_density_fitting ( method_str ) result ( wants_df ) !! Whether a method name asks for the fitted route !! !! \"ri-mp2\" and \"df-mp2\" parse to METHOD_TYPE_MP2 exactly as \"mp2\" does, !! so without this a deck asking for either would run the conventional !! four-index transform and report it as RI -- right answer, wrong method, !! and no way to tell from the output. Same reason `method_spin_scaling` !! exists: the spelling carries intent the type discards. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: method_str logical :: wants_df character ( len = :), allocatable :: lower_str integer :: i lower_str = trim ( adjustl ( method_str )) do i = 1 , len ( lower_str ) if ( lower_str ( i : i ) >= \"A\" . and . lower_str ( i : i ) <= \"Z\" ) then lower_str ( i : i ) = achar ( iachar ( lower_str ( i : i )) + 32 ) end if end do wants_df = ( index ( lower_str , \"ri-\" ) == 1 ) . or . ( index ( lower_str , \"df-\" ) == 1 ) end function method_wants_density_fitting","tags":"","url":"proc/method_wants_density_fitting.html"},{"title":"needs_serial_execution – metalquicha","text":"public pure function needs_serial_execution(method_type) result(serial) Whether this method has to be run on one thread An allow-list of the safe ones would be the tempting shape and the\nwrong one: a method added later would default to “safe” and inherit a\nsilent data race. Naming the unsafe ones means a new method is clamped\nuntil someone looks at it, which is the failure that costs an hour\nrather than a week. Lives here rather than in the driver because two places need it and one\nof them – the fragment workers – is used by the driver, so it cannot\nreach back. It used to be in the driver alone, and the worker path\nclamped everything instead of asking. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: method_type Return Value logical Called by proc~~needs_serial_execution~~CalledByGraph proc~needs_serial_execution needs_serial_execution proc~run_calculation run_calculation proc~run_calculation->proc~needs_serial_execution proc~set_worker_threads set_worker_threads proc~set_worker_threads->proc~needs_serial_execution proc~compute_energy_and_forces compute_energy_and_forces proc~compute_energy_and_forces->proc~run_calculation proc~gmbe_run_distributed gmbe_context_t%gmbe_run_distributed proc~gmbe_run_distributed->proc~set_worker_threads proc~mbe_run_distributed mbe_context_t%mbe_run_distributed proc~mbe_run_distributed->proc~set_worker_threads proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations->proc~run_calculation proc~run_step run_step proc~run_step->proc~run_calculation proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~run_calculation proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~run_calculation program~main main program~main->proc~run_calculation program~main->proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~run_geometry_optimization run_geometry_optimization program~main->proc~run_geometry_optimization proc~evaluate_energy_gradient evaluate_energy_gradient proc~evaluate_energy_gradient->proc~run_step proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~worker_loop worker_loop proc~worker_loop->proc~run_step proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~worker_loop~2->proc~worker_run proc~write_final_single_point write_final_single_point proc~write_final_single_point->proc~run_step proc~probe_gradient probe_gradient proc~probe_gradient->proc~evaluate_energy_gradient proc~run_geometry_optimization->proc~worker_loop proc~run_geometry_optimization->proc~write_final_single_point proc~run_geometry_optimization->proc~probe_gradient proc~session_begin mqc_session_t%session_begin proc~session_begin->proc~worker_loop~2 proc~mqc_session_begin mqc_session_begin proc~mqc_session_begin->proc~session_begin Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function needs_serial_execution ( method_type ) result ( serial ) !! Whether this method has to be run on one thread !! !! An allow-list of the safe ones would be the tempting shape and the !! wrong one: a method added later would default to \"safe\" and inherit a !! silent data race. Naming the unsafe ones means a new method is clamped !! until someone looks at it, which is the failure that costs an hour !! rather than a week. !! !! Lives here rather than in the driver because two places need it and one !! of them -- the fragment workers -- is used *by* the driver, so it cannot !! reach back. It used to be in the driver alone, and the worker path !! clamped everything instead of asking. integer , intent ( in ) :: method_type logical :: serial ! tblite. Threaded it corrupts a result rather than stopping, which is ! why this is not merely a performance choice. serial = ( method_type == METHOD_TYPE_GFN1 . or . method_type == METHOD_TYPE_GFN2 ) end function needs_serial_execution","tags":"","url":"proc/needs_serial_execution.html"},{"title":"parse_method_string – metalquicha","text":"public  function parse_method_string(method_str) result(method_type) Parse method string from input file (e.g., “XTB-GFN1” -> gfn1) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: method_str Return Value integer(kind=int32) Calls proc~~parse_method_string~~CallsGraph proc~parse_method_string parse_method_string proc~method_type_from_string method_type_from_string proc~parse_method_string->proc~method_type_from_string Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~parse_method_string~~CalledByGraph proc~parse_method_string parse_method_string proc~populate_config populate_config proc~populate_config->proc~parse_method_string proc~read_json_config_file read_json_config_file proc~read_json_config_file->proc~populate_config proc~read_json_config_text read_json_config_text proc~read_json_config_text->proc~populate_config proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~read_json_config_text proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~read_json_config_text program~main main program~main->proc~read_json_config_file proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~worker_loop~2->proc~worker_run proc~session_begin mqc_session_t%session_begin proc~session_begin->proc~worker_loop~2 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: dash_pos integer, private :: i character(len=:), private, allocatable :: lower_str character(len=:), private, allocatable :: method_part Source Code function parse_method_string ( method_str ) result ( method_type ) !! Parse method string from input file (e.g., \"XTB-GFN1\" -> gfn1) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: method_str integer ( int32 ) :: method_type character ( len = :), allocatable :: lower_str , method_part integer :: dash_pos , i ! Convert to lowercase allocate ( character ( len = len_trim ( method_str )) :: lower_str ) lower_str = trim ( adjustl ( method_str )) do i = 1 , len ( lower_str ) if ( lower_str ( i : i ) >= \"A\" . and . lower_str ( i : i ) <= \"Z\" ) then lower_str ( i : i ) = achar ( iachar ( lower_str ( i : i )) + 32 ) end if end do ! Handle \"XTB-GFN1\" format -> extract \"gfn1\" if ( index ( lower_str , \"xtb\" ) > 0 ) then dash_pos = index ( lower_str , \"-\" ) if ( dash_pos > 0 ) then method_part = lower_str ( dash_pos + 1 :) else method_part = lower_str end if else method_part = lower_str end if method_type = method_type_from_string ( method_part ) end function parse_method_string","tags":"","url":"proc/parse_method_string.html"},{"title":"method_spin_scaling – metalquicha","text":"public  subroutine method_spin_scaling(method_str, use_scs, ss_scale, os_scale) The spin-component scaling a method name implies, if any “scs-mp2” and “sos-mp2” both parse to METHOD_TYPE_MP2, so the type\nalone cannot tell them from plain MP2 and a deck asking for either\nwould run unscaled and report it as scaled. The spelling is the only\nplace the intent survives, so it is read here, beside the parser that\nalready has the lowercased string. SCS-MP2 is Grimme: 1.2 opposite-spin, one third same-spin. SOS-MP2 is\nHead-Gordon: 1.3 opposite-spin with the same-spin term dropped, which\nis what makes it cheaper rather than merely different. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: method_str logical, intent(out) :: use_scs real(kind=dp), intent(out) :: ss_scale real(kind=dp), intent(out) :: os_scale Called by proc~~method_spin_scaling~~CalledByGraph proc~method_spin_scaling method_spin_scaling proc~populate_config populate_config proc~populate_config->proc~method_spin_scaling proc~read_json_config_file read_json_config_file proc~read_json_config_file->proc~populate_config proc~read_json_config_text read_json_config_text proc~read_json_config_text->proc~populate_config proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~read_json_config_text proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~read_json_config_text program~main main program~main->proc~read_json_config_file proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~worker_loop~2->proc~worker_run proc~session_begin mqc_session_t%session_begin proc~session_begin->proc~worker_loop~2 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: i character(len=:), private, allocatable :: lower_str Source Code subroutine method_spin_scaling ( method_str , use_scs , ss_scale , os_scale ) !! The spin-component scaling a method name implies, if any !! !! \"scs-mp2\" and \"sos-mp2\" both parse to METHOD_TYPE_MP2, so the type !! alone cannot tell them from plain MP2 and a deck asking for either !! would run unscaled and report it as scaled. The spelling is the only !! place the intent survives, so it is read here, beside the parser that !! already has the lowercased string. !! !! SCS-MP2 is Grimme: 1.2 opposite-spin, one third same-spin. SOS-MP2 is !! Head-Gordon: 1.3 opposite-spin with the same-spin term dropped, which !! is what makes it cheaper rather than merely different. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: method_str logical , intent ( out ) :: use_scs real ( dp ), intent ( out ) :: ss_scale , os_scale character ( len = :), allocatable :: lower_str integer :: i lower_str = trim ( adjustl ( method_str )) do i = 1 , len ( lower_str ) if ( lower_str ( i : i ) >= \"A\" . and . lower_str ( i : i ) <= \"Z\" ) then lower_str ( i : i ) = achar ( iachar ( lower_str ( i : i )) + 32 ) end if end do use_scs = . false . ss_scale = 1.0_dp os_scale = 1.0_dp select case ( lower_str ) case ( \"scs-mp2\" ) use_scs = . true . os_scale = 1.2_dp ss_scale = 1.0_dp / 3.0_dp case ( \"sos-mp2\" ) use_scs = . true . os_scale = 1.3_dp ss_scale = 0.0_dp case default ! Every other spelling, including plain \"mp2\", is unscaled. The values ! set above the select already say so; this is here to be explicit ! that no scaling is the deliberate answer rather than a gap. end select end subroutine method_spin_scaling","tags":"","url":"proc/method_spin_scaling.html"},{"title":"mqc_run – metalquicha","text":"public  function mqc_run(system, terms, settings_len, settings, label_len, label, write_output, energy) result(status) bind(C, name=\"mqc_run\") Compute, and give the caller back a number terms may be null, which means “generate the list yourself” – the\nordinary fragmented run, with distance screening and sorting. A list\nthat is supplied is taken as given, in the order given, and must be\nclosed under subsets; run_calculation refuses one that is not. energy is the total. The per-term breakdown is not returned, because\nit is what a screening pass wants and it is already written to the\nfragment breakdown file – which is why write_output and a returned\nresult are independent rather than a single switch. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), value :: system type(c_ptr), value :: terms May be null: generate the list instead integer(kind=c_int), value :: settings_len character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in) :: settings (settings_len) integer(kind=c_int), value :: label_len character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in) :: label (label_len) integer(kind=c_int), value :: write_output real(kind=c_double), intent(out) :: energy Return Value integer(kind=c_int) Calls proc~~mqc_run~~CallsGraph proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~chars_to_string chars_to_string proc~mqc_run->proc~chars_to_string proc~current_session current_session proc~mqc_run->proc~current_session proc~energy_total energy_t%energy_total proc~mqc_run->proc~energy_total proc~error_get_message error_t%error_get_message proc~mqc_run->proc~error_get_message proc~error_has_error error_t%error_has_error proc~mqc_run->proc~error_has_error proc~refuse_undeclared_cuts refuse_undeclared_cuts proc~mqc_run->proc~refuse_undeclared_cuts proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~mp2_total mp2_energy_t%mp2_total proc~energy_total->proc~mp2_total proc~missing_broken_bonds missing_broken_bonds proc~refuse_undeclared_cuts->proc~missing_broken_bonds proc~session_run->proc~error_has_error proc~apply_log_level apply_log_level proc~session_run->proc~apply_log_level proc~bcast_string bcast_string proc~session_run->proc~bcast