m an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function fraglist_level_of ( this , iterm ) result ( level ) !! How many monomers a term actually has !! !! The store is padded to `max_level`, so the order of a term is the !! count of its non-zero entries rather than the number of rows. class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: iterm integer ( default_int ) :: level level = 0 if ( iterm < 1 . or . iterm > this % n_terms ) return level = count ( this % terms ( iterm , :) > 0 ) end function fraglist_level_of","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_level_of.html"},{"title":"add_unique – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine add_unique(store, n_stored, max_level, candidate, level) Append a term unless an identical one is already present Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(inout) :: store (:,:) integer(kind=int64), intent(inout) :: n_stored integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: max_level integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: candidate (:) integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: level Called by proc~~add_unique~~CalledByGraph proc~add_unique add_unique proc~fraglist_close_subsets fraglist_t%fraglist_close_subsets proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~add_unique proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets mqc_fraglist_close_subsets proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets->proc~fraglist_close_subsets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), private :: istored Source Code subroutine add_unique ( store , n_stored , max_level , candidate , level ) !! Append a term unless an identical one is already present integer ( default_int ), intent ( inout ) :: store (:, :) integer ( int64 ), intent ( inout ) :: n_stored integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: max_level integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: candidate (:) integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: level integer ( int64 ) :: istored do istored = 1 , n_stored if ( count ( store ( istored , :) > 0 ) /= level ) cycle if ( all ( store ( istored , 1 : level ) == candidate ( 1 : level ))) return end do n_stored = n_stored + 1 store ( n_stored , :) = 0 store ( n_stored , 1 : level ) = candidate ( 1 : level ) end subroutine add_unique","tags":"","url":"proc/add_unique.html"},{"title":"first_combination – metalquicha","text":"private pure subroutine first_combination(indices, k) The lexicographically first choice of k positions Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(inout) :: indices (:) integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: k Called by proc~~first_combination~~CalledByGraph proc~first_combination first_combination proc~fraglist_close_subsets fraglist_t%fraglist_close_subsets proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~first_combination proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets mqc_fraglist_close_subsets proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets->proc~fraglist_close_subsets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i Source Code pure subroutine first_combination ( indices , k ) !! The lexicographically first choice of k positions integer ( default_int ), intent ( inout ) :: indices (:) integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: k integer ( default_int ) :: i do i = 1 , k indices ( i ) = i end do end subroutine first_combination","tags":"","url":"proc/first_combination.html"},{"title":"fraglist_close_subsets – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_close_subsets(this, error) Add back every subset of every surviving term A screened list is not usable until this has run. An n-body term’s\ncontribution is its energy less the delta of every proper subset, so\nkeeping a trimer whose dimers fell below the threshold does not\napproximate anything – the assembly looks the subsets up and fails.\nScreening therefore selects which high-order terms to keep, and this\nrestores what computing them requires. That is also why an energy screen saves less than it first appears: a\ntrimer kept for its own sake drags in three dimers and three monomers,\nmost of which the screen had already rejected on their own account.\nThe saving is real but it is in the tail, not the whole list. Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error Calls proc~~fraglist_close_subsets~~CallsGraph proc~fraglist_close_subsets fraglist_t%fraglist_close_subsets proc~add_unique add_unique proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~add_unique proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~error_set proc~first_combination first_combination proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~first_combination proc~fraglist_level_of fraglist_t%fraglist_level_of proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~fraglist_level_of proc~next_index_set next_index_set proc~fraglist_close_subsets->proc~next_index_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~fraglist_close_subsets~~CalledByGraph proc~fraglist_close_subsets fraglist_t%fraglist_close_subsets proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets mqc_fraglist_close_subsets proc~mqc_fraglist_close_subsets->proc~fraglist_close_subsets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), private :: capacity integer(kind=default_int), private, allocatable :: closed (:,:) logical, private :: has_next integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=int64), private :: iterm integer(kind=default_int), private :: level integer(kind=int64), private :: n_closed integer(kind=default_int), private :: size_wanted integer(kind=default_int), private :: subset (MAX_TERM_LEVEL) integer(kind=default_int), private :: term (MAX_TERM_LEVEL) Source Code subroutine fraglist_close_subsets ( this , error ) !! Add back every subset of every surviving term !! !! **A screened list is not usable until this has run.** An n-body term's !! contribution is its energy less the delta of every proper subset, so !! keeping a trimer whose dimers fell below the threshold does not !! approximate anything -- the assembly looks the subsets up and fails. !! Screening therefore selects which *high-order* terms to keep, and this !! restores what computing them requires. !! !! That is also why an energy screen saves less than it first appears: a !! trimer kept for its own sake drags in three dimers and three monomers, !! most of which the screen had already rejected on their own account. !! The saving is real but it is in the tail, not the whole list. class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ) :: error integer ( default_int ), allocatable :: closed (:, :) integer ( default_int ) :: subset ( MAX_TERM_LEVEL ), term ( MAX_TERM_LEVEL ) integer ( int64 ) :: iterm , n_closed , capacity integer ( default_int ) :: level , size_wanted , i logical :: has_next if ( this % n_terms <= 0 ) return if ( this % max_level > MAX_TERM_LEVEL ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , & \"fraglist: max_level exceeds what subset closure supports\" ) return end if ! Every term of order n contributes at most 2&#94;n - 1 subsets including ! itself; duplicates are dropped below, so this is a bound, not a count. capacity = this % n_terms * int ( 2 ** this % max_level , int64 ) allocate ( closed ( capacity , this % max_level )) closed = 0 n_closed = 0 do iterm = 1 , this % n_terms term = 0 level = this % level_of ( iterm ) term ( 1 : level ) = this % terms ( iterm , 1 : level ) do size_wanted = 1 , level call first_combination ( subset , size_wanted ) do call add_unique ( closed , n_closed , this % max_level , & [( term ( subset ( i )), i = 1 , size_wanted )], size_wanted ) call next_index_set ( subset , size_wanted , level , has_next ) if (. not . has_next ) exit end do end do end do if ( allocated ( this % distances )) deallocate ( this % distances ) this % has_distances = . false . call move_alloc ( closed , this % terms ) this % n_terms = n_closed end subroutine fraglist_close_subsets","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_close_subsets.html"},{"title":"fraglist_create – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_create(this, n_monomers, max_level, error) Every combination of monomers from 2 up to max_level Monomers themselves are not terms here. The MBE evaluates them, but\nthey are implied by the monomer count and generate_fragment_list starts at pairs; a caller that wants them in the list can add them. Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: n_monomers integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: max_level type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error Calls proc~~fraglist_create~~CallsGraph proc~fraglist_create fraglist_t%fraglist_create proc~create_monomer_list create_monomer_list proc~fraglist_create->proc~create_monomer_list proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~fraglist_create->proc~error_set proc~fraglist_destroy fraglist_t%fraglist_destroy proc~fraglist_create->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~generate_fragment_list generate_fragment_list proc~fraglist_create->proc~generate_fragment_list proc~get_nfrags get_nfrags proc~fraglist_create->proc~get_nfrags proc~combine combine proc~generate_fragment_list->proc~combine proc~binomial binomial proc~get_nfrags->proc~binomial proc~combine_util combine_util proc~combine->proc~combine_util proc~combine_util->proc~combine_util Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~fraglist_create~~CalledByGraph proc~fraglist_create fraglist_t%fraglist_create proc~mqc_fraglist_generate mqc_fraglist_generate proc~mqc_fraglist_generate->proc~fraglist_create Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), private :: capacity integer(kind=int64), private :: count integer(kind=default_int), private, allocatable :: monomers (:) Source Code subroutine fraglist_create ( this , n_monomers , max_level , error ) !! Every combination of monomers from 2 up to max_level !! !! Monomers themselves are not terms here. The MBE evaluates them, but !! they are implied by the monomer count and `generate_fragment_list` !! starts at pairs; a caller that wants them in the list can add them. class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: n_monomers integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: max_level type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ) :: error integer ( default_int ), allocatable :: monomers (:) integer ( int64 ) :: capacity , count call this % destroy () if ( n_monomers <= 0 ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , \"fraglist: a system needs at least one monomer\" ) return end if if ( max_level < 2 ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , & \"fraglist: max_level must be at least 2 to have any n-mer terms\" ) return end if if ( max_level > n_monomers ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , & \"fraglist: max_level exceeds the number of monomers\" ) return end if ! get_nfrags counts monomers too, so it is an upper bound rather than the ! exact size -- generous by n_monomers entries, which is nothing beside ! the n-mer count and saves counting the combinations twice. capacity = get_nfrags ( n_monomers , max_level ) allocate ( monomers ( n_monomers )) call create_monomer_list ( monomers ) allocate ( this % terms ( capacity , max_level )) this % terms = 0 count = 0_int64 call generate_fragment_list ( monomers , max_level , this % terms , count ) deallocate ( monomers ) this % max_level = max_level this % n_terms = count end subroutine fraglist_create","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_create.html"},{"title":"fraglist_destroy – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_destroy(this) Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this Called by proc~~fraglist_destroy~~CalledByGraph proc~fraglist_destroy fraglist_t%fraglist_destroy proc~check_supplied_terms check_supplied_terms proc~check_supplied_terms->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~fraglist_replace fraglist_t%fraglist_replace proc~check_supplied_terms->proc~fraglist_replace proc~fraglist_create fraglist_t%fraglist_create proc~fraglist_create->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~fraglist_replace->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~mqc_fraglist_free mqc_fraglist_free proc~mqc_fraglist_free->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~run_fragmented_calculation run_fragmented_calculation proc~run_fragmented_calculation->proc~fraglist_destroy proc~run_fragmented_calculation->proc~fraglist_replace proc~mqc_fraglist_generate mqc_fraglist_generate proc~mqc_fraglist_generate->proc~fraglist_create proc~mqc_fraglist_set mqc_fraglist_set proc~mqc_fraglist_set->proc~fraglist_replace proc~run_calculation run_calculation proc~run_calculation->proc~run_fragmented_calculation proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~check_supplied_terms proc~session_run->proc~run_calculation proc~compute_energy_and_forces compute_energy_and_forces proc~compute_energy_and_forces->proc~run_calculation proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations->proc~run_calculation proc~run_step run_step proc~run_step->proc~run_calculation proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~run_calculation program~main main program~main->proc~run_calculation program~main->proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~evaluate_energy_gradient evaluate_energy_gradient proc~evaluate_energy_gradient->proc~run_step proc~worker_loop worker_loop proc~worker_loop->proc~run_step proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~worker_loop~2->proc~worker_run proc~write_final_single_point write_final_single_point proc~write_final_single_point->proc~run_step proc~probe_gradient probe_gradient proc~probe_gradient->proc~evaluate_energy_gradient proc~run_geometry_optimization run_geometry_optimization proc~run_geometry_optimization->proc~worker_loop proc~run_geometry_optimization->proc~write_final_single_point proc~session_begin mqc_session_t%session_begin proc~session_begin->proc~worker_loop~2 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine fraglist_destroy ( this ) class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this if ( allocated ( this % terms )) deallocate ( this % terms ) if ( allocated ( this % distances )) deallocate ( this % distances ) this % max_level = 0 this % n_terms = 0 this % has_distances = . false . end subroutine fraglist_destroy","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_destroy.html"},{"title":"fraglist_keep – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_keep(this, mask, error) Keep the terms the mask selects, in their existing order The one operation every screen reduces to. Distance screening, an\nenergy threshold read out of a previous run’s CSV, and resuming from\nthe terms a dead run never reached are the same call with a different\nmask – which is the point of the list being an object. Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this logical, intent(in) :: mask (:) One entry per term type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error Calls proc~~fraglist_keep~~CallsGraph proc~fraglist_keep fraglist_t%fraglist_keep proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~fraglist_keep->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), private :: iterm integer(kind=default_int), private, allocatable :: kept (:,:) real(kind=dp), private, allocatable :: kept_distances (:) integer(kind=int64), private :: n_kept Source Code subroutine fraglist_keep ( this , mask , error ) !! Keep the terms the mask selects, in their existing order !! !! The one operation every screen reduces to. Distance screening, an !! energy threshold read out of a previous run's CSV, and resuming from !! the terms a dead run never reached are the same call with a different !! mask -- which is the point of the list being an object. class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this logical , intent ( in ) :: mask (:) !! One entry per term type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ) :: error integer ( default_int ), allocatable :: kept (:, :) real ( dp ), allocatable :: kept_distances (:) integer ( int64 ) :: iterm , n_kept if ( size ( mask , kind = int64 ) /= this % n_terms ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , & \"fraglist: the mask does not have one entry per term\" ) return end if n_kept = count ( mask , kind = int64 ) allocate ( kept ( max ( n_kept , 1_int64 ), this % max_level )) kept = 0 if ( this % has_distances ) allocate ( kept_distances ( max ( n_kept , 1_int64 ))) n_kept = 0 do iterm = 1 , this % n_terms if (. not . mask ( iterm )) cycle n_kept = n_kept + 1 kept ( n_kept , :) = this % terms ( iterm , :) if ( this % has_distances ) kept_distances ( n_kept ) = this % distances ( iterm ) end do call move_alloc ( kept , this % terms ) if ( this % has_distances ) call move_alloc ( kept_distances , this % distances ) this % n_terms = n_kept end subroutine fraglist_keep","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_keep.html"},{"title":"fraglist_measure – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_measure(this, sys_geom, error) Compute the minimum inter-monomer distance of every term Separate from create because a list that arrived from a caller needs\nit too, and because a caller screening on energy never needs it at all\n– it is O(n_terms * atoms&#94;2) and worth not paying for. Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this type( system_geometry_t ), intent(in) :: sys_geom type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error Calls proc~~fraglist_measure~~CallsGraph proc~fraglist_measure fraglist_t%fraglist_measure proc~calculate_fragment_distances calculate_fragment_distances proc~fraglist_measure->proc~calculate_fragment_distances proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~fraglist_measure->proc~error_set proc~to_angstrom to_angstrom proc~calculate_fragment_distances->proc~to_angstrom Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine fraglist_measure ( this , sys_geom , error ) !! Compute the minimum inter-monomer distance of every term !! !! Separate from `create` because a list that arrived from a caller needs !! it too, and because a caller screening on energy never needs it at all !! -- it is O(n_terms * atoms&#94;2) and worth not paying for. class ( fraglist_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this type ( system_geometry_t ), intent ( in ) :: sys_geom type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ) :: error if ( this % n_terms <= 0 ) then call error % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , \"fraglist: nothing to measure, the list is empty\" ) return end if if ( allocated ( this % distances )) deallocate ( this % distances ) allocate ( this % distances ( this % n_terms )) call calculate_fragment_distances ( this % terms , this % n_terms , sys_geom , this % distances ) this % has_distances = . true . end subroutine fraglist_measure","tags":"","url":"proc/fraglist_measure.html"},{"title":"fraglist_replace – metalquicha","text":"private  subroutine fraglist_replace(this, terms, n_terms, max_level, error) Take a caller’s term list wholesale Whatever was known about the old list – distances above all – does\nnot describe the new one, so it is dropped rather than left to be read\nas though it did. Type Bound fraglist_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( fraglist_t ), intent(inout) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: terms (:,:) (n_terms, max_level) integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: n_terms integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: max_level type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error Calls proc~~fraglist_replace~~CallsGraph proc~fraglist_replace fraglist_t%fraglist_replace proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~fraglist_replace->proc~error_set proc~fraglist_destroy fraglist_t%fraglist_destroy proc~fraglist_replace->proc~fraglist_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~fraglist_replace~~CalledByGraph proc~fraglist_replace fraglist_t%fraglist_replace proc~check_supplied_terms check_supplied_terms proc~check_supplied_terms->proc~fraglist_replace proc~mqc_fraglist_set mqc_fraglist_set proc~mqc_fraglist_set->proc~fraglist_replace proc~run_fragmented_calculation run_fragmented_calculation proc~run_fragmented_calculation->proc~fraglist_replace proc~run_calculation run_calculation proc~run_calculation->proc~run_fragmented_calculation proc~session_run mqc_session_t%session_run proc~session_run->proc~check_supplied_terms proc~session_run->proc~run_calculation proc~compute_energy_and_forces compute_energy_and_forces proc~compute_energy_and_forces->proc~run_calculation proc~mqc_run mqc_run proc~mqc_run->proc~session_run proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations->proc~run_calculation proc~run_step run_step proc~run_step->proc~run_calculation proc~worker_run worker_run proc~worker_run->proc~run_calculation program~main main program~main->proc~run_calculation program~main->proc~run_multi_molecule_calculations proc~evaluate_energy_gradient evaluate_energy_gradient proc~evaluate_energy_gradient->proc~run_step proc~worker_loop worker_loop proc~worker_loop->proc~run_step proc~worker_loop~2 worker_loop proc~wor