nnecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private, parameter :: MESSAGE_LEN = 512 integer(kind=c_int), private, parameter :: MQC_BAD_HANDLE = 2 integer(kind=c_int), private, parameter :: MQC_FAIL = 1 integer(kind=c_int), private, parameter :: MQC_OK = 0 character(len=MESSAGE_LEN), private, save :: last_message = \"\" Functions public  function mqc_run (system, terms, settings_len, settings, label_len, label, write_output, energy) result(status) bind(C, name=\"mqc_run\") Compute, and give the caller back a number Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), value :: system type(c_ptr), value :: terms May be null: generate the list instead integer(kind=c_int), value :: settings_len character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in) :: settings (settings_len) integer(kind=c_int), value :: label_len character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in) :: label (label_len) integer(kind=c_int), value :: write_output real(kind=c_double), intent(out) :: energy Return Value integer(kind=c_int) private pure function chars_to_string (n, chars) result(text) A C character array as a Fortran string Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), intent(in) :: n character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(in) :: chars (n) Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Subroutines public  subroutine mqc_run_last_error (buffer_len, buffer) bind(C, name=\"mqc_run_last_error\") Copy the most recent failure message out as a C string Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=c_int), value :: buffer_len character(kind=c_char, len=1), intent(inout) :: buffer (buffer_len) private  subroutine refuse_undeclared_cuts (h, status) Refuse a partition that cuts bonds nobody declared Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( system_handle_t ), intent(in) :: h integer(kind=c_int), intent(out) :: status","tags":"","url":"module/mqc_capi_run.html"},{"title":"mqc_lebedev – metalquicha","text":"Lebedev-Laikov angular quadrature on the unit sphere A Lebedev grid is a union of orbits of the octahedral group: pick a seed\npoint, apply every coordinate permutation and sign flip, and every point\nproduced carries the same weight. So the whole grid – 5810 points at the\nlargest order – is stored as 1287 (code, a, b, weight) records in mqc_lebedev_data , and expanded here. The six orbit types differ only in their seed: code 0   (1, 0, 0)                        6 points\n   code 1   (0, s, s)      s = sqrt(1/2)    12 points\n   code 2   (t, t, t)      t = sqrt(1/3)     8 points\n   code 3   (a, a, b)      b = sqrt(1-2a&#94;2) 24 points\n   code 4   (a, b, 0)      b = sqrt(1-a&#94;2)  24 points\n   code 5   (a, b, c)      c = sqrt(1-a&#94;2-b&#94;2)  48 points Rather than tabulate each orbit’s points, expand_orbit generates them\nfrom the seed and discards duplicates – which is where the smaller counts\ncome from, a seed with a repeated or zero coordinate having a smaller\norbit than the 48 of a general point. The expected size is asserted, so a\nseed that collapsed further than it should cannot pass silently. Weights are normalised to sum to 1: the 4*pi of the sphere’s surface is\nnot included and must be supplied by the caller if it is wanted.\nThe orders that exist\nWhether a given point count is one of them\nSmallest available order >= a request\nWhether an order carries negative weights\nPoints and weights for an order Uses mqc_error pic_types mqc_lebedev_data module~~mqc_lebedev~~UsesGraph module~mqc_lebedev mqc_lebedev module~mqc_error mqc_error module~mqc_lebedev->module~mqc_error module~mqc_lebedev_data mqc_lebedev_data module~mqc_lebedev->module~mqc_lebedev_data pic_types pic_types module~mqc_lebedev->pic_types module~mqc_lebedev_data->pic_types Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~mqc_lebedev~~UsedByGraph module~mqc_lebedev mqc_lebedev module~mqc_dft_grid mqc_dft_grid module~mqc_dft_grid->module~mqc_lebedev Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=dp), private, parameter :: DUPLICATE_TOL = 1.0e-12_dp Two generated points closer than this are the same point. Seeds differ in\ntheir parameters by far more than this, so no distinct pair can merge. integer, private, parameter :: MAX_ORBIT_POINTS = 48 Largest orbit: a seed with three distinct non-zero coordinates reaches\nall 6 permutations in all 8 sign combinations. integer, private, parameter :: NEGATIVE_WEIGHT_ORDERS (3) = [74, 230, 266] Orders whose weights are not all positive. These are valid quadratures – they integrate to the stated accuracy, and\ntheir weights still sum to 1 – but a negative weight can drive a\nnumerically integrated density negative in a low-density region, so DFT\ngrids conventionally avoid them. Checked against PySCF: the same three\norders, with 8, 6 and 18 negative weights respectively. integer, private, parameter :: N_DIM = 3 Spatial dimensions integer, private, parameter :: N_ORBIT_CODES = 6 Number of orbit types, codes 0 to N_ORBIT_CODES-1 integer, private, parameter :: N_PERMUTATIONS = 6 Permutations of three coordinates integer, private, parameter :: ORBIT_SIZE (0:N_ORBIT_CODES-1) = [6, 12, 8, 24, 24, MAX_ORBIT_POINTS] Points in the orbit of each code, before duplicates are removed Functions public pure function lebedev_available_orders () result(orders) Available Lebedev orders, ascending Arguments None Return Value integer, (size(LEBEDEV_ORDERS)) public pure function lebedev_has_negative_weights (n_points) result(negative) Whether this order carries negative weights Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: n_points Return Value logical public pure function lebedev_is_available (n_points) result(available) Whether n_points is one of the Lebedev orders Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: n_points Return Value logical public pure function lebedev_order_at_least (n_points) result(order) Smallest available order with at least n_points points Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: n_points Return Value integer private pure function orbit_seed (code, a, b) result(seed) The seed point of an orbit, on the unit sphere by construction Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: code real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: a real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: b Return Value real(kind=dp), (3) Subroutines public  subroutine lebedev_grid (n_points, points, weights, error) Unit-sphere quadrature points and weights for one Lebedev order Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: n_points Must be an available order real(kind=dp), intent(out), allocatable :: points (:,:) (3, n_points), on the unit sphere real(kind=dp), intent(out), allocatable :: weights (:) Sums to 1 type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: error private pure subroutine expand_orbit (code, a, b, points, n_points) Every distinct image of one orbit’s seed under the octahedral group Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: code Orbit type, 0 to 5 real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: a Seed parameters; unused for codes 0 to 2 real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: b Seed parameters; unused for codes 0 to 2 real(kind=dp), intent(out) :: points (N_DIM,MAX_ORBIT_POINTS) Distinct images, first n_points valid integer, intent(out) :: n_points","tags":"","url":"module/mqc_lebedev.html"},{"title":"mqc_elements – metalquicha","text":"Provides atomic numbers, element symbols, and atomic masses for the complete\nperiodic table (elements 1-118) with conversion functions between representations.\nConvert element symbol to atomic number\nConvert atomic number to element symbol\nGet atomic mass by atomic number\nGet covalent radius by atomic number Uses pic_ascii pic_types module~~mqc_elements~~UsesGraph module~mqc_elements mqc_elements pic_ascii pic_ascii module~mqc_elements->pic_ascii pic_types pic_types module~mqc_elements->pic_types Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~mqc_elements~~UsedByGraph module~mqc_elements mqc_elements module~mqc_bond_perception mqc_bond_perception module~mqc_bond_perception->module~mqc_elements module~mqc_physical_fragment mqc_physical_fragment module~mqc_bond_perception->module~mqc_physical_fragment module~mqc_config_adapter mqc_config_adapter module~mqc_config_adapter->module~mqc_elements module~mqc_config_adapter->module~mqc_physical_fragment module~mqc_config_types mqc_config_types module~mqc_config_adapter->module~mqc_config_types module~mqc_method_config mqc_met